Make the CPU-only overclock cover the rest of the CPU, and widen VSync's
iteration-counted timeout to match.

Patch 0030 routed instruction charges, icache refills and load completion
through the crash2 clock. Three CPU-side costs still ran at 1x under "200%":

  - main-RAM read waits (3 cycles per load, memory.c psx_cyc_readmem). The
    PS1 has no data cache beyond the 1 KB scratchpad, so almost every load in a
    frame paid it. 0030 lumped RAM in with the genuine device waits.
  - GTE command latency (psx_gte_set). The GTE is COP2 on the CPU die; left
    unscaled, GTE-bound code gained nothing from the overclock and stalled
    slightly LONGER, because the instructions between a command and its read
    advanced the clock by half as much.
  - MULT/DIV latency (psx_muldiv_set). Same unit, same reasoning; DIV is 37.

Beetle, whose timing this runtime mirrors, arms all three in the CPU timestamp
domain, where its overclock shortens them. Real devices - SPU, CDC, GPU, MDEC,
DMA, timers - keep their own timing, as before. At 100% every change here is
the identity, so stock timing is bit-for-bit unchanged.

The RAM wait is a charge, so it goes through the shared carry exactly as 0030
did for load completion. GTE and MULT/DIV latencies are deadlines rather than
charges, so they use a stateless round-to-nearest scale instead: feeding a
deadline through the carry would nudge the rounding of the instruction charges
around it.

Consequence that had to ship with it: PsyQ's v_wait (0x8004A5CC) times out on
LOOP ITERATIONS, `t = timeout << 15`, and on expiry prints "VSync: timeout" and
calls ChangeClearPAD(0) / ChangeClearRCnt(3,0), changing how the kernel
acknowledges VBlank for the rest of the session. Per-iteration cost at a
fully-scaled 200% is about half of stock, so 32768 iterations covered ~0.76 of
a field and a frame finishing early could expire it before VBlank. The game
also calls VSync(5/20/60), whose first wait takes (n-1) << 15. While the clock
is raised crash2_60fps.h now widens both argument words inside VSync's own
range, so v_wait - the spin loop - stays native:

  0x8004A510  addiu a1,a0,-1 -> addu a1,a0,a0    (n-1 -> 2n)
  0x8004A534  addiu a1,zero,1 -> addiu a1,zero,2

Timeout = multiplier x cycles-per-iteration, and every cost in that loop now
scales with the clock, so x2 restores at least stock margin up to the 200% cap.
Raising C2_60_CPU_CAP past 200 must raise the multiplier with it. The words are
signature-guarded with the rest, restored whenever the clock returns to 100,
and re-applied if the game reloads its executable. The release heartbeat gains
native_60fps_vsync_wide so a run can prove the widening was in force.

No file here is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake: the codegen tag does not move,
the recompiler does not need rebuilding and the shard cache stays valid.
Overlay DLLs reach psx_muldiv_set, the GTE path and loads through g_cbs
forwarding stubs, so they pick the change up without re-emission.

--- a/runtime/src/psx_cycles.c
+++ b/runtime/src/psx_cycles.c
@@ -56,6 +56,28 @@
     return (uint32_t)(total >> 32);
 }
 
+/* Scale a CPU-side LATENCY - a completion deadline, not a charge.
+ *
+ * The GTE (COP2) and the MULT/DIV unit are on the R3000A die and run on the
+ * CPU clock, so a CPU overclock shortens how long they take exactly as it
+ * shortens an instruction. Beetle, whose timing this runtime mirrors, arms
+ * both deadlines in the CPU timestamp domain for the same reason.
+ *
+ * Deliberately NOT psx_crash2_cpu_clock_charge. That one carries fractional
+ * cycles between calls because its results are all advanced; a deadline is
+ * only advanced to if nothing else gets there first, so feeding it through
+ * the shared carry would nudge the rounding of the instruction charges around
+ * it. Round to nearest instead, with no state: at 200% RTPT's 22 becomes 11,
+ * DIV's 37 becomes 19, and a 1-cycle latency stays 1 rather than vanishing.
+ *
+ * At 100% this is the identity, so stock timing is bit-for-bit unchanged. */
+static inline uint32_t psx_crash2_cpu_clock_latency(uint32_t cycles) {
+    if (g_crash2_cpu_clock_pct == 100u || cycles == 0u) return cycles;
+    uint64_t scaled = ((uint64_t)cycles * s_crash2_cpu_clock_recip
+                       + (1ull << 31)) >> 32;
+    return scaled ? (uint32_t)scaled : 1u;
+}
+
 /* Native overlay DLLs batch their CPU charges before invoking this callback.
  * Share the same fractional carry as main-exe and interpreted instructions. */
